{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md; rg -l 'test-runner' .cpcache/*.cp; sed -n '1,70p' test/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc; rg -n 'spit|temp-path|create-content-file|delete|File\\\\.' test/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n.cpcache/3644903311.cp\n(ns yin.vm.debruijn-test\n  \"D0-D3 of docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md: the published\n   :yin.debruijn/* dimension with its hash domain, §5's canonical value\n   table and NFC seam, §2's root framing and input validation, §3's\n   scope resolution, D2's Merkle records — node hashes, the root\n   fingerprint, hash-consing, and the d5 storage adapter — and D3's\n   settled byte rules with pinned cross-host byte fixtures. The pinned\n   literals are asserted by the JVM, Node, and Dart lanes alike: one\n   byte string, three hosts.\"\n  (:require [clojure.test :refer [are deftest is testing]]\n            [dao.datom :as datom]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ring]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn :as d]\n            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Fixtures: AST builders, the emitter's datoms, and defect capture\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- lit\n  [v]\n  {:type :literal, :value v})\n\n\n(defn- v\n  [s]\n  {:type :variable, :name s})\n\n\n(defn- lam\n  [params body]\n  {:type :lambda, :params params, :body body})\n\n\n(defn- app\n  [op & args]\n  {:type :application, :operator op, :operands (vec args)})\n\n\n(defn- if-node\n  [t c a]\n  {:type :if, :test t, :consequent c, :alternate a})\n\n\n(defn- tail\n  [node]\n  (assoc node :tail? true))\n\n\n(defn- datoms-of\n  [ast]\n  (second (vm/ast->datoms-with-root ast)))\n\n\n(defn- project\n  [ast]\n  (d/project-datoms (datoms-of ast)))\n\n\n(defn- throws-data\n  \"The ex-data of the exception `thunk` throws, or nil when it returns.\"\n  [thunk]\n  (try (thunk) nil\n       (catch #?(:clj Exception :cljs js/Error :cljd Object) e\n         (or (ex-data e) {}))))\n\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
